  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for heavy rain/localized flooding into\ntonight \n\nA decision was made early on the evening shift to expand the\nFlash Flood Watch eastward across the north central mountains\nthrough Sullivan Co. This area has since been the focal point\nfor a training axis of heavy rainfall, producing over 5 inches\nof rain at IPT and surrounding areas. Flash flooding has been\nobserved in Lycoming and Union counties, and small streams and \ncreeks are still on the rise in Sullivan Co. The heavy rain also\nled to river flooding at Penns Creek in Union County. This\nprompted us to issue a River Flood Warning - the first river\nFLW since Jul 1, 2025 other than an FLW for ice jam flooding in\nFebruary of this year.\n\nA line of thunderstorms moving across the Laurels this evening\nhas generally been progressive in nature, dropping less than 1\ninch of rain over an hour in any given location, despite\nestimated instantaneous rainfall rates closer to 4\" per hour. We\nwill continue to monitor this feature closely as it translates\nnortheastward, especially as it approaches Union/Lycoming/Sullivan\ncounties, where saturated soils certainly would not be able to \nhandle much additional water. The good news is that CAPE does \ndecrease with northeastern extent.\n\nWe also continue to monitor the convection for areas of\nrotation. The LLJ has increased early this evening to 35 kts\nacross eastern PA (25 kts over our western zones). Several\nstorms have exhibited mostly weak rotation. We did issue a\ntornado warning for a rotating discrete cell in western Warren \nCounty early this evening that has since expired.\n\nFor now, the best heavy rain/severe wx signal is to the south \nand east of CPA tomorrow/Monday. Heading into the work week, \nlatest model QPF data now favors a break in the rain through \nearly week before daily shower/t-storm chances return Wed-Fri. \nSome localized heavy rain and marginal flooding risk will \ncontinue into late week.",
